response:
To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function or the `return` statement.

The `exit()` function is used to exit the entire process and return control to the operating system. It takes an integer argument that specifies the status code of the termination:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Your code here

    exit(0);  // Terminate with success (status code 0)
}
```

If you want to terminate your program without returning any value, you can use the `return` statement:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");

    return 0;  // Return 0 to indicate successful execution
}
```

In both cases, after calling either `exit()` or `return`, the program will immediately stop executing and return control to the operating system. The operating system then handles the termination of the process as it sees fit.
